---


// Screen tour of the actual build (public/demo.mp4, H.264 + AAC).
//
// WALKTHROUGH v4 (2026-08-22): the silent, card-captioned cut follows ONE
// order — the Alvarez-Whitman wedding in the Maple & Main demo world, named on
// screen in the opening beat — from the food cost that lands before the quote
// goes out, through the shop list and the prep list (the same order, one tab to
// the right each time), the ACTUAL email send (the capture inbox is on camera),
// an imperfect delivery, and the quoted-versus-today result. One inset partway
// through shows three events planned as a single production run. There is no
// speech track and there will not be one; a generated music bed plays and every
// claim is written on screen. The cards are burned into the frames by the
// capture step.
//
// v4 added the prep beat and the sub-recipe cards on the shop beat. The title
// card had promised "prep it" since the first cut and no frame had ever shown a
// prep list; the sub-recipe panel had been in every frame of the shop beat with
// no card mentioning it. Both are ledger-backed already (RC-24, RC-18) and both
// were verified against the seeded wedding before they were written: its
// Braised Short Rib carries Mirepoix Base and House Beef Stock as sub-recipes,
// so the claims and the through-line stay on the same dish.
//
// SIX THINGS HERE MOVE WITH THE FOOTAGE, EVERY TIME: the chip's duration, the
// sr-only transcript, the video's aria-label, the poster, the h2, and the hero
// link's duration (guard-pinned in check-landing-claims.mjs). They have
// drifted twice — commit 3e8d136 left the chip reading 48 sec over a 39.5s
// cut, and an earlier cut narrated a different menu than the order it quoted.
//
// THE MULTI-EVENT INSET CLAIMS PLANNING ONLY (RC-36). /orders/batch exposes
// on-hand entry and nothing else: confirming an order, freezing its prices and
// sending its purchase orders all stay per order. Nothing here, in the
// transcript or in the footage, may suggest the combined run confirms or buys.
//
// Regenerate (app repo first: `npm run demo:seed`, then `npm run demo:capture`,
// Mailpit up; then `./demo/serve.sh` there to leave a preview on :4181):
//   APP=http://localhost:4181 node scripts/demo-video/capture-silent.mjs
//   node scripts/demo-video/assemble-silent.mjs   (ffmpeg-static + playwright-core)
// The assembler prints the encoded duration and the three places that have to
// agree with it. See docs/demo-video-rebuild-2026-08-21.md.
---

	<section id="demo" class="container-page py-section-tight">
		<div data-reveal>
			<h2 class="max-w-3xl text-h2 font-semibold">
				Follow one 180-guest wedding from quote to shelf. Then three events in one run.
			</h2>
			<p class="mt-5 max-w-[68ch] text-lede text-ink-soft">
				This is the working product, not a mockup. Two and a half minutes: the food
				cost that lands before the quote goes out, the shopping and prep lists it turns
				into, purchase orders that really send, a delivery that arrives wrong, and the
				price carried back to the next job.
			</p>
		<!-- WCAG 1.2.1 and CLAUDE.md principle 4: this stands in for the footage
		     for anyone who cannot watch it, so it describes the cut IN ORDER and
		     carries every figure the cards carry. It is also a truth-pass
		     surface, scanned against the forbidden-claims list like everything
		     else in this file. Rewrite it whenever the cut is re-recorded. -->
		<p id="demo-transcript" class="sr-only">
				A silent, captioned screen recording of CostCook following one catering order
				from quote to shelf. There is no narration; a music bed plays and every claim
				is written on screen, so nothing is carried by audio alone. A title card:
				cost it, buy it, prep it, pack it, enter the numbers once. A year of jobs on
				the books, with the next one named: the Alvarez-Whitman wedding, 180 guests,
				three days out. The order&rsquo;s pricing panel: 180 guests at $68 a head is $12,240.00 of revenue
				against $4,847.96 of food, which is 39.6 percent food cost against a 30
				percent target, and the panel names $89.78 a guest as the price that would
				meet it, all before the quote is sent. The same order as a shopping list
				grouped by vendor in the whole packs you actually buy, Baldor totalling
				$690.82, beside a sub-recipe panel that treats the stock and the mirepoix as
				batches to make rather than as things to shop for: eight batches of Mirepoix
				Base, six of House Beef Stock, with an on-hand entry that reduces the
				purchasing when you have already made some. The same order&rsquo;s prep list:
				thirty-four items scaled to the job, 75.6 kilograms of boneless beef short rib
				among them, sub-recipes carried as their own tagged lines, and a check-off box
				on every row that keeps its state. An aside: three events in the same week,
				280 guests between them,
				planned as one production run and submitted together as three orders, opening
				on a combined Shop, Prep and Pack workspace that totals the shopping and the
				prep across all three while the pack list keeps a by-event column so each
				event&rsquo;s trays stay separate. Each order still gets its own confirmation
				and its own purchase orders; the combined run does not confirm or buy. Back on
				the wedding: a capture inbox showing the purchase orders the confirmation
				actually emailed, one per vendor, each with its own number, and the one
				addressed to Green Valley Produce opened as the vendor receives it, five items
				in whole packs with a $516.08 subtotal. Receiving: arugula received three of
				four three-pound clamshells, the missing clamshell saved for purchasing
				follow-up; baby spinach ordered as fifteen three-pound clamshells and
				delivered as sixteen, $477.16 of received value against the $410.40 ordered.
				The ingredient&rsquo;s cost panel showing the price actually paid as the
				current cost, $21.91 a kilogram, with the receiving it came from on record.
				Finally the order&rsquo;s comparison band: quoted at $26.93 a guest, $27.13 at
				today&rsquo;s prices, a 0.7 percent rise with the quote still holding. An end card: one job, quote to shelf, the same numbers
				the whole way.
			</p>

		<!-- Not styled as a ticket: real product footage should read as real,
		     not as another branded artifact. Poster is the frame that names the
		     price: the ring on "Charge at least $89.78 per guest", with $68.00
		     and $12,240.00 either side of it. It was the vendor-grouped shop
		     list, which is a dense table that reads as a spreadsheet screenshot
		     at the width a phone actually draws this player. Chosen by card in
		     assemble-silent.mjs, so re-timing b02 moves it too. -->
		<!-- Centred and capped at the capture width: the footage is 1600x1000, and
		     stretching it past that only blurs it. -->
		<div class="relative mt-8 mx-auto max-w-[1600px]">
			<video
				controls
				playsinline
				preload="none"
				poster="/demo-poster.jpg"
				width="1600"
				height="1000"
				class="w-full border border-hairline bg-paper shadow-ticket"
				aria-label="Silent, captioned screen recording: CostCook prices a 180-guest wedding before the quote goes out, turns it into a vendor shopping list and a scaled prep list, plans three events as one production run, emails its purchase orders, and records a delivery that arrived wrong"
				aria-describedby="demo-transcript"
			>
				<source src="/demo.webm" type="video/webm" />
				<source src="/demo.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
				<p>
					Your browser can&rsquo;t play this video.
					<a href="/demo.mp4" class="btn-quiet">Download it instead</a>
				</p>
			</video>
			<!-- Play affordance: the poster alone reads as a static screenshot.
			     Dark chip echoes the footage's own caption chips. Decorative
			     (the video carries the labels), lets taps fall through to the
			     video, removed on first play; shown only when scripting can
			     remove it, so no-JS playback is never covered. -->
			<span
				id="demo-play-badge"
				class="pointer-events-none absolute top-3 left-3 hidden items-center gap-2 rounded-full bg-ink/85 px-3.5 py-1.5 text-sm font-medium text-offwhite shadow-ticket [@media(scripting:enabled)]:inline-flex"
				aria-hidden="true"
			>
				<svg width="10" height="12" viewBox="0 0 10 12" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
					<path d="M0 0l10 6-10 6z"></path>
				</svg>
				Watch · 2 min 30 sec
			</span>
		</div>

		<!-- No CTA here on purpose. This section sits third now, and a Start link
		     at this depth was one of six competing next-actions. The reader is
		     still being shown the thing; the ask belongs at the close. -->
	</div>
</section>

<script>
	// One job: clear the play chip once the footage is rolling.
	const video = document.querySelector<HTMLVideoElement>('#demo video');
	const badge = document.getElementById('demo-play-badge');
	if (video && badge) {
		video.addEventListener('play', () => badge.remove(), { once: true });
	}
</script>
